  "id": "Compound::DB00605 - Gene::5742",
  "verbalization": "Sulindac, a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory compound, is linked to Ptgs1 through multiple pathways that converge on the enzyme inhibitor and carbonyl compound classes. The evidence suggests that Sulindac, as part of the Nonsteroidal Anti-inflammatory Compounds class, binds to Ptgs1 via interactions with the enzyme inhibitor class, which is a recurring mechanism across different compounds in this class. This indicates a potential common mechanism of action involving enzyme inhibition or direct binding to the target protein. The lowest common ancestor of Sulindac and its downstream compounds, such as Nabumetone and Piroxicam, points to a shared structural feature—carbonyl compounds or organosulfur compounds—suggesting a possible structural basis for their interaction with Ptgs1. While the exact mechanism of binding remains unclear, the consistent presence of enzyme inhibitor properties across these compounds supports a broader role in modulating prostaglandin synthesis, likely through shared pathways or targets.",
